'''Liban Qaasim''' (born 12 July 1971) is a Burawi politician and former investment banker who has served as [[Chairman of the Industrial Party of Burawa]] since 2021. He previously served as vice-chairman of the party from 2018 to 2021. He has represented [[Mazar]] in the [[Lower House of Burawa]] since 2011, first for [[Mazar-3]] and subsequently for [[Mazar-1]] following constituency reorganisation in 2020.

Qaasim is regarded as the principal advocate for commercial and private sector interests in Burawi politics. His negotiation of the 2012 Burawi Tax Reform Act, which introduced tax-free treatment for startup stock options until liquidity, established his reputation as an effective legislator for business-friendly policy. He played a pivotal role in the [[2026 Burawi leadership recall vote|2026 recall crisis]], negotiating an agreement with the administration of [[Hancil Thea]] to reduce corporate taxes from 30 per cent to 25 per cent in exchange for Industrial Party opposition to the recall.

He is the Industrial Party candidate in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|2026 snap leadership election]].

== Early life and education ==

Liban Qaasim was born on 12 July 1971 in [[Mazar]], Burawa's only city with private land ownership and the centre of its private financial sector. He completed his secondary education in Mazar before enrolling at the [[University of Mazar]], where he studied finance and economics.

== Early career ==

=== Ministry of the Treasury ===

In the 1990s, Qaasim worked within the [[Ministry of the Treasury (Burawa)|Ministry of the Treasury]] under the Socialist Party government. His tenure coincided with the party's long period of governance and provided him with direct experience in fiscal policy and public finance administration.

=== Mazar Industrial Bank ===

After leaving government service, Qaasim joined the '''Mazar Industrial Bank''', one of the largest private investment banks in Burawa. His work in private finance exposed him to the challenges faced by the commercial sector within Burawa's state-socialist economy and shaped his subsequent political advocacy for liberalisation.

== Political career ==

=== Entry into politics (2011) ===

Qaasim was elected to the [[Lower House of Burawa]] in the [[2011 Burawi Lower House election]], representing [[Mazar-3]] as a member of the [[Industrial Party of Burawa]]. His election reflected Mazar's distinctive political culture, which prioritised commercial interests and private sector development.

=== 2012 Burawi Tax Reform Act ===

{{main|2012 Burawi Tax Reform Act}}

In his first term, Qaasim authored and secured passage of the [[2012 Burawi Tax Reform Act]], which introduced a qualified tax deduction for startup stock options. Under the legislation, stock options granted to employees of qualifying startups were tax-free until liquidity — when the shares were sold — rather than at the time of grant. The reform was intended to incentivise startup formation and employee participation in high-growth companies, and it established Qaasim's reputation as an effective advocate for business-friendly policy within the socialist economic framework.

=== Rise within the Industrial Party ===

Qaasim became an influential figure within the Industrial Party from approximately 2008, building relationships with commercial leaders and establishing himself as the party's principal negotiator on matters affecting private enterprise. His banking background and Treasury experience gave him credibility on fiscal and financial policy.

In 2018, Qaasim was elected [[Vice-chairman of the Industrial Party of Burawa]]. In 2021, he succeeded [[Cumar Siyaad]] as [[Chairman of the Industrial Party of Burawa]], becoming the party's leader.

=== Constituency change (2020) ===

Following constituency reorganisation, Qaasim contested [[Mazar-1]] in the [[2020 Burawi Lower House election]] rather than Mazar-3. The new constituency encompassed the commercial core of Mazar, reinforcing his identity as the representative of Burawa's private sector.

=== 2026 recall crisis ===

{{main|2026 Burawi leadership recall vote}}

During the March 2026 leadership recall crisis, Qaasim played a central role as the Industrial Party's negotiator. The party abstained during the first recall vote on 13 March, withholding its 58 votes pending negotiations with the administration of [[Hancil Thea]].

On 19 March, Qaasim announced an agreement with the Thea administration: in exchange for Industrial Party opposition to the recall, the government would reduce the corporate tax rate on private enterprises from 30 per cent to 25 per cent for a period of five years, beginning in fiscal year 2027. The concession was valued at approximately 150 billion [[Burawi Hanti|hanti]] in foregone revenue annually.

The deal proved decisive. On 20 March, the Industrial Party's 58 members voted against the recall, contributing to the motion's failure to achieve the supermajority threshold. The agreement drew criticism from opposition parties, who characterised it as a "political bribe" to preserve Thea's position, while Qaasim defended it as a legitimate advancement of economic liberalisation.

== 2026 snap election campaign ==

{{main|2026 Burawi snap election}}

Following the successful recall of Thea and the scheduling of snap elections, Qaasim announced his candidacy for [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] as the Industrial Party nominee. His campaign emphasised economic liberalisation, private sector development, and the reduction of barriers to commercial enterprise within Burawa's socialist framework.

Qaasim participated in televised debates on 8 April and 12 April 2026, alongside the other candidates. His polling performance placed him behind the major candidates, reflecting the Industrial Party's niche role as a commercial interest advocate rather than a broad-based political movement.

== Political positions ==

Qaasim is associated with economic liberalisation and private sector advocacy within Burawa's state-socialist system.

On economic policy, Qaasim advocates for reduced corporate taxation, fewer restrictions on private enterprise, and expanded opportunities for commercial activity in sectors traditionally dominated by state-owned enterprises. He supports the classification of more industries as "non-essential" to permit private investment.

On labour policy, Qaasim has supported reforms to the Temporary Worker Program Act, arguing for flexibility in labour markets while maintaining worker protections.

On regional policy, Qaasim's focus on Mazar and commercial interests places him in opposition to the rural-focused policies of the National Party. He has argued that Burawa's economic growth depends on a vibrant private sector centred in Mazar.

On governance, Qaasim has defended the Industrial Party's negotiation strategy as pragmatic governance, arguing that incremental liberalisation through coalition-building is more effective than ideological opposition.

